2. All rights reserved — the engine
------------------------------------

Everything else in this repository, and in particular:

    src/lib/**          the audit engine, rule parser and classifier, the
                        deterministic check library, the judge and its
                        literal-evidence gate, ruleset health, the receipt
                        format, the preference extractor, and the licence system
    src/lib/prevent/**  the precondition, control and claim-check corpus — the
                        accumulated definitions of what an action requires, what
                        a control must prove, and which observation settles which
                        claim. Named separately because it is the part that grows
                        with use, and because a corpus is only an asset if the
                        terms on it are stated before it is worth copying.
    src/app/**          the web application
    tests/**            the test suite, including the gold set

is **Copyright (c) 2026 Enforcee. All rights reserved.**

It is published so that it can be read. It is not published so that it can be
reused. Specifically, no licence is granted to copy, modify, redistribute,
sublicense, or create derivative works from these files, or to use them — or the
methodology, rule taxonomy, evaluation prompts, thresholds or scoring they
contain — to build or train a competing product.

This mirrors a distinction the industry has already settled in practice rather
than in argument. Semgrep publishes its engine under LGPL 2.1 and puts its rules
under a separate licence, explicitly "to clarify that other vendors may not use
Semgrep Community Edition rules as part of a competing Software as a Service
offering." ESLint took the other path, could not sustain owning its rule corpus,
froze it at ~280 rules, and watched the ecosystem's value accrue to everyone
except ESLint. The engine here is readable for the reasons in section 1. The
corpus is reserved for the reasons in this one.

You may read it. You may quote short excerpts when discussing or reviewing it.
You may report bugs against it. You may run it locally to satisfy yourself that
it does what this repository says it does — that is the entire reason it is
visible.

Why the split, stated plainly: the deterministic engine and the evidence gate are
the product. Publishing them under a permissive licence would let a better-funded
competitor ship them next week with our own test suite as proof, and there would
be nothing left to sell. The parts you have to trust are open. The part we sell
is not. We would rather say that in a licence file than be vague about it in a
footer.


Contributions
-------------

Contributions to the MIT-licensed directories are welcome under the MIT Licence.
By opening a pull request against those paths you agree your contribution is
licensed the same way.

We do not accept pull requests against src/lib/**. Open an issue instead — bug
reports there are genuinely valuable and we would rather have the report than the
patch.


Questions
---------

If you want to do something this file does not clearly allow, ask rather than
guess: 8patriksz@gmail.com. If your use is non-commercial, educational, or you are
a researcher benchmarking verification tools, the answer is very likely yes.
